#!/usr/bin/env srr-uvrun
"""SRR external pipeline step: prepend a self-hosted TTS narration to articles.

srr execs any non-#builtin `pipe` entry once per item, writing the item JSON
to stdin and reading the (possibly modified) item JSON back from stdout:

    {guid, title, content, link, published, lang?, raw, drop?}

`raw` (the parsed feed entry) is read-only: srr restores its own typed copy
over whatever comes back, so the reply omits the key entirely. `guid` and
`published` are immutable — changing either is a pipeline error, which costs
the article.

This step narrates title + body text with piper (fully offline) and prepends

    <p><audio controls preload="none" data-tts-t="0,4.2,..." src="#/tts/<hash>.wav"></audio></p>

The "#/"-marker file is written under the fetch run's asset dir; srr's
end-of-pipeline upload step ships it to the store content-addressed (and the
store-side asset processor — webify on this deployment — transcodes the WAV
to web audio before upload).

PARAGRAPH SYNC
--------------
The narration is synthesized per block segment, so each segment's exact
start offset is known (piper yields per-sentence chunks; counting frames
costs nothing). The audio tag carries the table as
data-tts-t="0,4.2,11.8,..." and every narrated block is stamped
data-tts="<segment index>" — segment 0 is the TITLE when there is one, and
the reader highlights its own title node for it. The SRR frontend uses the
pair to highlight the block being read and to seek on click; the backend's
#sanitize allowlists exactly these two attributes (digits / a comma-joined
decimal list). Start offsets are cached in a sidecar
(tts/<hash>.wav.json) next to the WAV; a cached WAV without one (pre-1.3
cache) degrades to a plain audio tag rather than re-synthesizing.

Opt a feed in (order matters: after #readability if the feed uses it, before
#default so the marker rides the normal sanitize/upload path):

    srr feed upd <id> -p 'srr-tts --voice es_ES-davefx-medium' -p '#default'

VOICE RESOLUTION (first hit wins)
---------------------------------
  1. --voice <name>          explicit piper voice, e.g. en_US-lessac-medium
  2. --lang-voice xx=voice   per-feed extension/override of the table (repeatable)
  3. built-in table          keyed by the item's `lang` field (en es fr de pt it)
  none resolvable -> the item passes through unchanged (logged to stderr).
  srr auto-detects `lang` BEFORE the pipeline runs (a declared value from
  the ingest strategy wins; short/uncertain text leaves it empty), so the
  language-keyed table works on plain feeds too. Use --voice to force a
  voice when detection stays empty or picks wrong.

The asset dir arrives as $SRR_ASSET_DIR (set by srr on the fetch path; absent
in `srr preview` and older backends -> pass through, never synthesize).
Synthesized WAVs are cached there under tts/ keyed by voice+text hash: a
cache hit only refreshes the file's mtime (so srr's age-based sweep keeps
live files) and never re-synthesizes.

ON THE FETCH PATH THIS STEP NEVER EXITS NON-ZERO. srr drops an item whose
pipe step fails and keeps its guid, so the article is gone for good — worse
than losing the narration. Every failure, INCLUDING a typo'd flag in the
feed's `pipe`, therefore reports on stderr and leaves the item alone. Run the
step manually (an HTML file argument) to have mistakes reported loudly with a
non-zero exit. --help/--version print on STDERR for the same reason: stdout
is the protocol channel, and non-JSON there fails srr's parse — dropping the
article as surely as a non-zero exit would.

KNOBS
-----
  --voice <name>            explicit piper voice (wins over everything)
  --lang-voice xx=voice     extend/override the language->voice table (repeatable)
  --max-chars <n>           narration cap, truncated at a sentence boundary;
                            0 disables it. The default (32768) is a backstop
                            real articles stay under (a full daily digest is
                            ~8k chars), bounding the damage of a pathological
                            one — NOT a guarantee against srr's timeout: srr
                            drops an item whose pipe step exits non-zero or
                            times out, and keeps its guid in BoundaryGUIDs,
                            so a --cmd-timeout kill loses the article
                            permanently (never retried). Measured (piper
                            medium voice, Latin prose): ~1000 chars per
                            MINUTE of speech (~17 chars/s), ~2.6 MB of WAV
                            per 1000 chars, and ~41s synthesis per 3000
                            chars + 2s model load on a 4-core ARM64 box (the
                            class that runs the fetch loop) — so an article
                            FILLING the default cap is ~33 min of audio /
                            ~85 MB / ~7.5 min of synthesis, past the default
                            5m --cmd-timeout. A feed that can genuinely hit
                            the cap needs a smaller one (~18000 fits the 5m
                            timeout with margin) or a raised --cmd-timeout.
                            First use also downloads the ~60 MB voice model
                            inside the SAME timeout (1.5s on a fast link; on
                            a slow one pre-warm the voice with a manual run
                            before opting a feed in). The cap counts
                            CHARACTERS and the ratios above are Latin-script:
                            a CJK voice packs far more speech per character,
                            so re-measure before sizing a cap there.
  --voices-dir <dir>        piper models dir (default $XDG_DATA_HOME else
                            ~/.local/share, + /srr-tts); models auto-download
                            there on first use. Deliberately OUTSIDE srr's
                            age-swept asset cache.
  --asset-dir <dir>         manual runs: where tts/ audio goes (what
                            $SRR_ASSET_DIR carries on the fetch path)

MANUAL RUNS (parameters instead of stdin)
-----------------------------------------
Pass an HTML file as argument instead of item JSON on stdin — the same item
dict is built from parameters and the result is pretty-printed:

    srr-tts --voice es_ES-davefx-medium --asset-dir /tmp/store article.html
    srr-tts --lang es --asset-dir /tmp/store --title "Titular" article.html

  <file>                    HTML file whose content becomes the item body
  --title <text>            item title (narrated first)
  --lang <xx>               item lang field (drives the voice table)
"""
import hashlib
import html
import json
import math
import os
import re
import shutil
import sys
import tempfile
import time
import wave

from bs4 import BeautifulSoup
from bs4.element import CData, NavigableString, PreformattedString, Tag

VERSION = "1.3"

DEFAULT_MAX_CHARS = 32_768  # roomy backstop, not timeout-proof: an article
# filling it outruns the default --cmd-timeout on the fetch-loop box class
# (the KNOBS entry owns the sizing math)

# language (lowercase primary subtag, the item's `lang` field) -> default
# piper voice. --lang-voice overrides/extends per feed; --voice bypasses it.
# All "medium" on purpose: one quality tier across languages, so the size and
# timing figures documented for --max-chars hold whichever voice is picked.
LANG_VOICES = {
    "en": "en_US-lessac-medium",
    "es": "es_ES-davefx-medium",
    "fr": "fr_FR-siwis-medium",
    "de": "de_DE-thorsten-medium",
    "pt": "pt_BR-faber-medium",
    "it": "it_IT-paola-medium",
}

# %s #1: either "" or the pre-escaped ' data-tts-t="…"' timing attribute;
# %s #2: the escaped src. The table rides the element (not a sidecar asset)
# so the reader needs no extra fetch and the store ships nothing new.
AUDIO_HTML = '<p><audio controls preload="none"%s src="%s"></audio></p>'

# piper's own voice-name grammar (download_voices.VOICE_PATTERN): the
# downloader REBUILDS the filename from these groups, so a name that doesn't
# match — or that carries stray whitespace, which it strips — makes it write
# files under a different name than we then look for. Validating at parse time
# turns "every item of this feed fails forever, one 60 MB download each" into
# one loud startup error.
VOICE_RE = re.compile(r"^[^-\s]+_[^-\s]+-[^-\s]+-\S+$")

# A voice model is downloaded into a .dl-* staging dir; a SIGKILL (srr's
# --cmd-timeout) skips the cleanup, and voices_dir is deliberately outside
# srr's age-swept cache, so nothing else reclaims them. Sweep our own debris,
# but only when it is far older than any live download could be.
STAGE_TTL = 6 * 3600

# After a failed load we re-download once, assuming a corrupt cache. But
# "failed to load" also covers environmental faults (OOM on a small box, fd
# exhaustion) that a re-download cannot fix — and srr runs one process per
# item, so nothing in-process remembers a failed heal. The model's mtime is
# that memory: a model touched more recently than this was already healed,
# so don't pull 60 MB again for every item of the feed.
HEAL_COOLDOWN = 3600


def log(*a):
    print("srr-tts:", *a, file=sys.stderr)


def fnv1a32(s: str) -> int:
    h = 0x811C9DC5
    for b in s.encode("utf-8"):
        h = ((h ^ b) * 0x01000193) & 0xFFFFFFFF
    return h


# Subtrees that must never reach the narration (content is pre-#sanitize
# here: scripts/styles may still be present; audio/video keeps a re-run from
# narrating its own player markup) and elements whose boundaries read as
# paragraph breaks. Inline tags (a, b, span, ...) flow into the sentence.
DROP_TAGS = ("script", "style", "noscript", "template", "iframe",
             "audio", "video", "svg", "math", "head")
BLOCK_TAGS = ("p", "div", "li", "ul", "ol", "blockquote", "pre", "table",
              "tr", "figure", "figcaption", "article", "section", "aside",
              "header", "footer", "details", "summary",
              "h1", "h2", "h3", "h4", "h5", "h6",
              "dl", "dt", "dd", "td", "th", "caption")


_BLOCK_SET = frozenset(BLOCK_TAGS)
_DROP_SET = frozenset(DROP_TAGS)

_WS_RUN = re.compile(r"[ \t\r\f\v]+")
_NL_PAD = re.compile(r" ?\n ?")
_NL_RUN = re.compile(r"\n{3,}")


def _collapse(text: str) -> str:
    text = _WS_RUN.sub(" ", text)
    text = _NL_PAD.sub("\n", text)
    return _NL_RUN.sub("\n\n", text).strip()


def segment_html(fragment: str):
    """HTML fragment -> (soup, [(element_or_None, text), ...]) in document
    order: one segment per block-bounded text run, attributed to the
    innermost still-open block element (None outside any block). Empty
    segments are dropped. "\\n\\n".join of the texts IS html_to_text — block
    boundaries read as paragraph breaks either way; the attribution is what
    lets run_item stamp the narrated element for the reader's
    highlight-follow. Attribute text (alt, URLs) is never included; comments
    and other non-text strings are skipped, CDATA text is kept (get_text
    parity).

    The returned soup is PRISTINE: the walker only READS it, never mutates
    it (no decompose, no replace_with). run_item re-serializes this same
    soup as the stored article content, so anything the walker skips for
    narration purposes — a <br>, a DROP_TAGS subtree — must still be there
    in the output: <br> as itself, not a literal newline; <video>/<audio>
    players intact, not gone."""
    soup = BeautifulSoup(fragment or "", "html.parser")
    segs = [[None, []]]  # [owning element or None, text parts]

    # Recurses one Python frame per nesting level (unlike the old iterative
    # get_text) and so can hit RecursionError on a pathologically deep
    # fragment (~1000+ levels). Survivable, not handled here: it's an
    # Exception, so main()'s run_item backstop catches it and emits the item
    # unchanged — that article loses its narration, never the article itself.
    def walk(node, cur):
        for child in node.children:
            if isinstance(child, Tag):
                if child.name in _DROP_SET:
                    continue  # never narrated — but stays in the soup as-is
                if child.name == "br":
                    segs[-1][1].append("\n")  # a break, not a subtree: no walk()
                    continue
                if child.name in _BLOCK_SET:
                    segs.append([child, []])
                    walk(child, child)
                    segs.append([cur, []])  # text after it re-attributes to cur
                else:
                    walk(child, cur)
            elif isinstance(child, NavigableString) and (
                    not isinstance(child, PreformattedString)
                    or isinstance(child, CData)):
                segs[-1][1].append(str(child))

    walk(soup, None)
    out = []
    for el, parts in segs:
        text = _collapse("".join(parts))
        if text:
            out.append((el, text))
    return soup, out


def html_to_text(fragment: str) -> str:
    """HTML fragment -> plain text. Thin join over segment_html — one code
    path, so the narrated text and the per-segment attribution can never
    disagree."""
    return "\n\n".join(t for _, t in segment_html(fragment)[1])


def extract_text(title: str, content_html: str) -> str:
    """Item title + content HTML -> the plain text to narrate. '' when there
    is nothing to say.

    The title goes through the same conversion as the body: a feed title is
    NOT plain text — it routinely carries entities ("Tom &amp; Jerry",
    "It&#8217;s") and sometimes markup, and srr only unescapes and sanitizes
    it AFTER the pipeline, so this step always sees the raw form. Narrating
    that verbatim makes piper read the entity out loud. Collapsed to one line
    because a title is one utterance.

    run_item builds the same text via segment_html for per-segment
    attribution."""
    body = html_to_text(content_html)
    title = " ".join(html_to_text(title).split())
    if title and body:
        return title + "\n\n" + body
    return title or body


SENTENCE_END = re.compile(r"[.!?…](?=\s)")


def truncate(text: str, cap: int):
    """-> (text, truncated?). Over-cap text is cut at the last sentence end
    past 60% of the cap, else hard at the cap (a boundary too early would
    silently drop most of the article)."""
    if cap <= 0 or len(text) <= cap:
        return text, False
    cut = text[:cap]
    ends = [m.end() for m in SENTENCE_END.finditer(cut)]
    if ends and ends[-1] >= int(cap * 0.6):
        return cut[: ends[-1]].rstrip(), True
    return cut.rstrip(), True


def split_kept(texts, cap):
    """Map truncate() of the joined narration back onto its segments:
    -> the per-segment KEPT texts, a (possibly partial-tailed) prefix of
    `texts`. Segments the cut dropped entirely are absent, so table indices
    stay aligned with list positions."""
    joined = "\n\n".join(texts)
    cut, _ = truncate(joined, cap)
    kept, pos = [], 0
    for t in texts:
        if pos >= len(cut):
            break
        piece = cut[pos:pos + len(t)].rstrip()
        if piece:
            kept.append(piece)
        pos += len(t) + 2  # the "\n\n" separator
    return kept


def _tts_attr(starts):
    """starts -> the ' data-tts-t="…"' attribute. %.10g (not the more
    obvious %g, which is 6 significant digits: above 10000s it silently
    drops the second decimal, and at >=999999.5 it switches to exponent
    notation ("1e+06"), which would fail the backend's attr regex and kill
    the whole attribute) drops trailing zeros ("4.0" -> "4") while keeping
    all the precision round() left at synthesis time. Unreachable at sane
    --max-chars caps, but cheap to get right."""
    return ' data-tts-t="%s"' % ",".join("%.10g" % s for s in starts)


def read_sidecar(path):
    """-> starts list, or None if absent/unreadable/malformed (pre-upgrade
    cache, foreign file, or a future format this version doesn't know) — any
    of which must degrade to a plain tag, never raise. bool is checked
    separately because bool IS an int in Python (isinstance(True, int) is
    True) and would otherwise sneak past the numeric check."""
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            data = json.load(fh)
        if data.get("v") != 1:
            return None
        starts = data["starts"]
        if (isinstance(starts, list) and starts and all(
                isinstance(s, (int, float)) and not isinstance(s, bool)
                and math.isfinite(s) and s >= 0 for s in starts)
                and all(b >= a for a, b in zip(starts, starts[1:]))):
            return starts
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError, AttributeError):
        pass
    return None


def write_sidecar(path, starts):
    """Atomic sibling write, same .part+rename discipline as the WAV."""
    tmp = "%s.%d.part" % (path, os.getpid())
    try:
        with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            json.dump({"v": 1, "starts": starts}, fh)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    finally:
        if os.path.exists(tmp):
            try:
                os.remove(tmp)
            except OSError:
                pass


def audio_name(voice: str, text: str) -> str:
    """Store-relative name for a narration: content-addressed by voice+text
    so identical inputs reuse the same cache file (and dedup in the store)."""
    h = hashlib.sha256((voice + "\n" + text).encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()
    return "tts/%s.wav" % h[:32]


def default_voices_dir() -> str:
    # XDG data dir, NOT srr's asset cache: the cache is age-swept, voice
    # models are not per-run artifacts.
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_DATA_HOME") or os.path.join(
        os.path.expanduser("~"), ".local", "share")
    return os.path.join(base, "srr-tts")


def norm_lang(s: str) -> str:
    """Fold a language tag to the bare lowercase primary subtag ('ES' -> 'es',
    'pt-BR' -> 'pt'), mirroring the backend's normalizeLang. srr's own
    detection already emits bare codes, but the wire protocol lets an ingest
    strategy DECLARE lang (an RSS <language> copied verbatim) and srr never
    folds that onto the item — so fold here, or a declared 'pt-BR' silently
    misses the table."""
    return re.split(r"[-_]", s.strip().lower(), maxsplit=1)[0]


def resolve_voice(cfg: dict, language: str):
    """--voice > --lang-voice/built-in table by language > None."""
    if cfg["voice"]:
        return cfg["voice"]
    return cfg["lang_voices"].get(norm_lang(language or ""))


def fetch_voice(voice: str, voices_dir: str, onnx: str, conf: str):
    """Download a voice's two files into voices_dir, atomically.

    piper's own downloader skips any file that merely exists and is non-empty
    and writes straight to the destination, so a run killed mid-download —
    srr's --cmd-timeout can land there, the model is ~60 MB — would leave a
    truncated .onnx that is NEVER re-downloaded and fails load every cycle
    after. So stage into a sibling temp dir and os.replace each file into
    place (same filesystem, so the rename is atomic). Two feeds racing on a
    voice's first use both stage their own copy; last replace wins, same
    bytes.

    Note this covers the KILL case, not a silently short HTTP body: piper
    checks neither Content-Length nor the md5 upstream publishes, so a dropped
    connection still returns "success". ensure_voice's load-failure heal is
    what catches that.

    The piper import stays inside — see ensure_voice."""
    from pathlib import Path
    from piper.download_voices import download_voice
    os.makedirs(voices_dir, exist_ok=True)
    log("downloading voice %s -> %s" % (voice, voices_dir))
    stage = tempfile.mkdtemp(dir=voices_dir, prefix=".dl-")
    try:
        download_voice(voice, Path(stage))
        for dest in (onnx, conf):
            os.replace(os.path.join(stage, os.path.basename(dest)), dest)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(stage, ignore_errors=True)


def sweep_stale_stages(voices_dir: str):
    """Reclaim .dl-* staging dirs an interrupted run left behind. fetch_voice's
    finally cannot run on SIGKILL, and voices_dir sits outside srr's age-swept
    cache, so nothing else reclaims them. Called from ensure_voice on EVERY
    load, not just ahead of a download: in the common kill-then-retry sequence
    the retry succeeds minutes later — debris still younger than STAGE_TTL —
    and once the voice is complete a download-path-only sweep would never run
    again, making that ~60 MB leak permanent. Only dirs older than STAGE_TTL
    go, so a concurrent worker's live download is never pulled out from under
    it."""
    cutoff = time.time() - STAGE_TTL
    try:
        names = os.listdir(voices_dir)
    except OSError:
        return
    for name in names:
        if not name.startswith(".dl-"):
            continue
        path = os.path.join(voices_dir, name)
        try:
            stale = os.path.getmtime(path) < cutoff
        except OSError:
            continue
        if stale:
            log("removing stale download staging dir %s" % path)
            shutil.rmtree(path, ignore_errors=True)


def ensure_voice(voice: str, voices_dir: str):
    """Load the piper voice, downloading its model on first use. Imports stay
    inside: onnxruntime is heavy and pass-through runs must not pay for it.

    A voice is two files (PiperVoice.load derives the .onnx.json config from
    the model path) and both must be complete. Missing files are the cheap
    case; a file that is present but CORRUPT is not detectable by stat, so a
    load failure is treated as a poisoned cache and healed by one
    re-download. Without that, anything that predates the staged download
    above — or a short HTTP body, which piper does not detect — wedges the
    voice permanently: every cycle would fail load and pass the item through.

    The heal is rate-limited by the model's own mtime (HEAL_COOLDOWN),
    because a load failure is not proof of corruption: OOM on a small box
    fails the same way, and re-downloading 60 MB for every item of the feed
    would turn a memory problem into a bandwidth one."""
    from piper import PiperVoice
    sweep_stale_stages(voices_dir)
    onnx = os.path.join(voices_dir, voice + ".onnx")
    conf = onnx + ".json"
    if not (os.path.exists(onnx) and os.path.exists(conf)):
        fetch_voice(voice, voices_dir, onnx, conf)
    try:
        return PiperVoice.load(onnx)
    except Exception as e:
        try:
            healed_recently = time.time() - os.path.getmtime(onnx) < HEAL_COOLDOWN
        except OSError:
            healed_recently = False
        if healed_recently:
            raise  # freshly fetched and still broken — a re-download won't fix it
        log("voice %s failed to load (%s): re-downloading" % (voice, e))
        fetch_voice(voice, voices_dir, onnx, conf)
        return PiperVoice.load(onnx)


def synthesize(voice_obj, texts, dest):
    """Synthesize the segment texts as ONE WAV at dest; -> [start_seconds
    per segment], measured by counting emitted frames (piper yields one
    chunk per sentence; chunks are 16-bit mono, so frames = bytes/2).
    .part + rename so a killed run never leaves a truncated file for a later
    cycle (or a concurrent worker) to reuse. The temp name is PID-suffixed
    so two concurrent workers synthesizing the same voice+text can't tear
    each other's write (last os.replace wins, harmless — same bytes)."""
    rate = voice_obj.config.sample_rate
    tmp = "%s.%d.part" % (dest, os.getpid())
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dest), exist_ok=True)
    starts, frames = [], 0
    try:
        with wave.open(tmp, "wb") as w:
            w.setframerate(rate)
            w.setsampwidth(2)
            w.setnchannels(1)
            for text in texts:
                starts.append(round(frames / rate, 2))
                for chunk in voice_obj.synthesize(text):
                    w.writeframes(chunk.audio_int16_bytes)
                    frames += len(chunk.audio_int16_bytes) // 2
        os.replace(tmp, dest)
    finally:
        if os.path.exists(tmp):
            try:
                os.remove(tmp)
            except OSError:
                pass
    return starts


def run_item(item: dict, cfg: dict) -> dict:
    """One item through the step. Narration is an enhancement: every per-item
    problem logs to stderr and returns the item unchanged — never fail the
    feed's cycle.

    On success the reply also carries the reader's narration-sync markup:
    each narrated block is stamped data-tts="<segment index>", and the audio
    tag's data-tts-t lists every segment's start second (segment 0 is the
    title when there is one — the reader highlights its own title node for
    it). Both are junk to any other consumer and inert until the backend's
    #sanitize allowlists them. Content is only rewritten on this path — every
    pass-through returns the item byte-identical."""
    asset_dir = cfg["asset_dir"]
    if not asset_dir:
        log("no asset dir (preview or old backend): passing item through")
        return item
    soup, segs = segment_html(item.get("content") or "")
    title = " ".join(html_to_text(item.get("title") or "").split())
    narr = ([(None, title)] if title else []) + segs
    texts = [t for _, t in narr]
    if not texts:
        log("nothing to narrate: passing item through")
        return item
    kept = split_kept(texts, cfg["max_chars"])
    if len(kept) < len(texts) or (kept and kept[-1] != texts[len(kept) - 1]):
        log("narration truncated to %d segments (cap %d)"
            % (len(kept), cfg["max_chars"]))
    voice = resolve_voice(cfg, item.get("lang") or "")
    if not voice:
        log("no voice for language %r: passing item through"
            % (item.get("lang") or ""))
        return item
    # Same cache key as ever: voice + the truncated JOINED text.
    rel = audio_name(voice, "\n\n".join(kept))
    dest = os.path.join(asset_dir, rel)
    sidecar = dest + ".json"
    if os.path.exists(dest) and os.path.getsize(dest) > 0:
        # Refresh mtimes so srr's post-cycle age-based cache sweep never
        # deletes files a live feed still consumes (srr-youtube's pattern).
        os.utime(dest, None)
        starts = read_sidecar(sidecar)
        if starts:
            os.utime(sidecar, None)
        else:
            # Pre-upgrade cache: the WAV is good, the table is gone. Degrade
            # to the old plain tag rather than burn minutes re-synthesizing —
            # cache hits must NEVER re-synthesize.
            log("no timing sidecar for cached %s: plain audio tag" % rel)
            item["content"] = (AUDIO_HTML
                               % ("", html.escape("#/" + rel, quote=True))
                               + (item.get("content") or ""))
            return item
    else:
        try:
            starts = synthesize(ensure_voice(voice, cfg["voices_dir"]),
                                kept, dest)
        except Exception as e:  # any failure degrades — never fails the cycle
            log("synthesis failed (%s): %s" % (voice, e))
            return item
        try:
            write_sidecar(sidecar, starts)
        except Exception as e:
            # The WAV already landed and starts is in hand — losing THIS
            # run's table over a sidecar write failure would be needless.
            # Only a future cache hit degrades (to a plain tag, same as the
            # pre-upgrade-cache path above).
            log("sidecar write failed (%s): future cache hits will degrade "
                "to a plain tag" % e)
    # Feed-supplied sync attrs would misdirect the reader's highlight/seek;
    # ours are the only truth for this narration, so sweep any before
    # stamping.
    for el in soup.find_all(attrs={"data-tts": True}):
        del el["data-tts"]
    for el in soup.find_all(attrs={"data-tts-t": True}):
        del el["data-tts-t"]
    # Stamp the narrated blocks. Indices are positions in `kept`, title
    # included; a segment whose element is gone (bare interstitial text) or
    # already claimed (a parent block that opened two runs) keeps its table
    # entry but no stamp — the reader just skips the highlight there. (Index
    # width is sanitize-bounded to 4 digits by the backend's attr regex —
    # unreachable outside a pathological 10000+-block article.)
    for i, (el, _) in enumerate(narr[:len(kept)]):
        if el is not None and "data-tts" not in el.attrs:
            el["data-tts"] = str(i)
    item["content"] = (AUDIO_HTML
                       % (_tts_attr(starts),
                          html.escape("#/" + rel, quote=True))
                       + str(soup))
    return item


CFG_PARAMS = {"--voice": "voice", "--max-chars": "max_chars",
              "--voices-dir": "voices_dir", "--asset-dir": "asset_dir"}
ITEM_PARAMS = {"--title": "title", "--lang": "lang"}


def check_voice(name: str, errors: list) -> str:
    """Validate a voice name against piper's grammar, at parse time. piper
    only rejects it at first synthesis — which, since srr runs one process
    per item, means a silent per-item failure forever (and, for a name that
    merely has stray whitespace, a completed 60 MB download thrown away each
    time)."""
    if not VOICE_RE.match(name):
        errors.append("%r is not a piper voice name "
                      "(want lang_REGION-name-quality, e.g. en_US-lessac-medium)"
                      % name)
    return name


def parse_argv(argv):
    """-> (cfg, item-or-None). Manual mode (an HTML file argument) builds the
    same item dict srr sends as JSON on stdin (the toolbox's same-request
    rule); protocol mode returns item=None and main reads stdin.

    Argv errors are COLLECTED, not raised, because what a bad flag should do
    depends on the mode and we only know the mode once the whole argv is
    read. A human at a terminal wants a non-zero exit; the fetch path must
    never get one — srr drops an item whose step exits non-zero and never
    retries it, and it does not validate external steps up front, so one
    typo'd flag in a feed's `pipe` would silently destroy every article that
    feed ever publishes. In protocol mode a config error therefore degrades
    exactly like a per-item failure: complain on stderr, change nothing.

    Which mode we are in is decided by whether the file argument OPENS (see
    below), not by whether one was supplied — a mis-parsed flag value looks
    exactly like a filename, and guessing wrong costs articles."""
    cfg = {"voice": "", "max_chars": DEFAULT_MAX_CHARS,
           "voices_dir": default_voices_dir(),
           "asset_dir": os.environ.get("SRR_ASSET_DIR", ""),
           "lang_voices": dict(LANG_VOICES), "error": ""}
    errors = []
    manual = {}
    paths = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a in CFG_PARAMS or a in ITEM_PARAMS or a == "--lang-voice":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                errors.append("%s needs a value" % a)
                break
            val = argv[i + 1]
            if a == "--lang-voice":
                lang, sep, v = val.partition("=")
                if not sep or not lang or not v:
                    errors.append("--lang-voice wants xx=voice, got %r" % val)
                else:
                    cfg["lang_voices"][norm_lang(lang)] = check_voice(v, errors)
            elif a == "--voice":
                cfg["voice"] = check_voice(val, errors)
            elif a in CFG_PARAMS:
                cfg[CFG_PARAMS[a]] = val
            else:
                manual[ITEM_PARAMS[a]] = val
            i += 2
        elif a.startswith("-"):
            errors.append("unknown option %r (see --help)" % a)
            i += 1
        else:
            paths.append(a)
            i += 1
    try:
        cfg["max_chars"] = int(cfg["max_chars"])
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        errors.append("--max-chars must be an integer, got %r" % cfg["max_chars"])
        cfg["max_chars"] = DEFAULT_MAX_CHARS
    if cfg["max_chars"] < 0:
        # truncate() treats any cap <= 0 as "no cap"; a negative almost
        # certainly means a typo, and reading it as "no cap" would quietly
        # defeat the one thing a cap is set for. Say so.
        errors.append("--max-chars cannot be negative (0 disables the cap)")
    if not paths and manual:
        errors.append("--title/--lang need an HTML file argument "
                      "(protocol mode reads the item from stdin)")
    # Manual mode is confirmed by OPENING the file, never by merely seeing a
    # positional — an unknown flag's orphaned value ("--voce es_ES-x-medium")
    # is indistinguishable from a filename, and a stray token in a feed's
    # `pipe` looks like one too. Reading a file is the only evidence a human
    # is driving this, so it is the only thing that may authorize a non-zero
    # exit; anything else degrades to the fetch-safe path. The FIRST positional
    # that OPENS wins, because that orphaned value sits ahead of the real
    # filename in the natural ordering ("--voce X article.html").
    content = None
    open_err = None
    for candidate in paths:
        try:
            with open(candidate, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
                content = fh.read()
            break
        except OSError as e:
            if open_err is None:
                open_err = e   # the FIRST failure: the message names paths[0]
    if paths and content is None:
        errors.append("cannot read %s: %s" % (paths[0], open_err))
    elif len(paths) > 1 and not errors:
        errors.append("more than one input file (%s)"
                      % ", ".join(repr(p) for p in paths))
    if errors:
        if content is not None:
            sys.exit("srr-tts: " + errors[0])   # confirmed manual run
        cfg["error"] = errors[0]
        return cfg, None
    if content is None:
        return cfg, None
    item = {
        "guid": fnv1a32(content),
        "title": manual.get("title", ""),
        "content": content,
        "link": "",
        "published": None,
    }
    if manual.get("lang"):
        item["lang"] = manual["lang"]
    return cfg, item


def main():
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    # Help/version report on STDERR: stdout is srr's protocol channel, and
    # their text is not JSON — on the fetch path it would fail srr's parse and
    # drop the article as surely as a non-zero exit would. A stray help token
    # in a feed's `pipe` must degrade like every other mistake: loud on
    # stderr, empty stdout (= item unchanged).
    if argv and argv[0] in ("-h", "--help", "help"):
        print(__doc__, file=sys.stderr)
        return
    if argv and argv[0] in ("version", "--version", "-V"):
        print("srr-tts", VERSION, file=sys.stderr)
        return
    cfg, item = parse_argv(argv)
    pretty = item is not None
    if cfg["error"]:
        # Protocol mode only (a manual run already exited). Emitting nothing
        # is srr's documented "leave the item unchanged" — the one response
        # that neither drops the article nor pretends we narrated it.
        log("%s: passing items through unchanged" % cfg["error"])
        return
    if item is None:
        # The wire item is UTF-8 (Go's json encoder emits nothing else): pin
        # the decode so the service unit's locale gets no vote — a strict
        # UTF-8 locale would crash on invalid bytes (UnicodeDecodeError → a
        # dropped article), a coerced C locale would smuggle them through as
        # lone surrogates srr's decoder then mangles. ValueError covers that
        # crash and plain bad JSON; RecursionError covers a hostile `raw` —
        # srr caps XML nesting nowhere and Go ships arbitrary depth, but
        # Python's JSON scanner gives up near depth 500. Either way emit
        # nothing, which srr reads as "leave the item unchanged".
        try:
            sys.stdin.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="strict")
            item = json.load(sys.stdin)
        except (ValueError, RecursionError) as e:
            log("could not parse item on stdin (%s): emitting nothing" % e)
            return
    try:
        result = run_item(item, cfg)
    except Exception as e:
        # Absolute backstop: run_item mutates item["content"] only as the
        # final statement of its branch (the pre-upgrade-cache plain-tag
        # path and the stamped/tabled success path), so on any raise before
        # that point (e.g. a cache-hit TOCTOU between the getsize/utime
        # checks and a concurrent evictor) the item is still pristine —
        # emit it untouched rather than exit non-zero and have srr drop the
        # article.
        log("unexpected error: %s: passing item through" % e)
        result = item
    if pretty:
        json.dump(result, sys.stdout, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)
        print()
        return
    # `raw` is dropped, not echoed: srr saves the item's typed Raw payload
    # before unmarshaling our reply and restores it immediately after, so
    # whatever we send there is discarded either way. Echoing it re-encodes
    # the feed entry's whole XML tree once per item — a tree whose depth
    # nothing bounds (srr models it as a recursively nested map) — onto the
    # one channel that must never carry a partial write. Sending the key at
    # all is pure cost.
    #
    # Serialize BEFORE writing: json.dump streams the encoder's chunks
    # straight out, so a failure mid-encode leaves PARTIAL JSON on stdout,
    # which srr reads as a failed step — and the article is gone for good.
    # Buffering into one string first makes the reply all-or-nothing; on any
    # failure emit nothing, srr's "leave the item unchanged". allow_nan is
    # off for the same reason: the default emits bare NaN/Infinity, which is
    # not JSON and fails srr's parse just as a truncated body would.
    try:
        result.pop("raw", None)
        out = json.dumps(result, allow_nan=False)
    except Exception as e:
        log("could not serialize item (%s): emitting nothing" % e)
        return
    sys.stdout.write(out)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
